ives.\0Apub let String.reverse (s: String): String = Array.concat_all(Array.reverse(chars_of(s)))\0A/// Whether the string has no bytes.\0Apub let String.is_empty (s: String): Bool = s.len() == 0\0A/// Characters from `start` up to but not including `end`, counted in\0A/// CODEPOINTS rather than bytes. Out-of-range bounds are clamped.\0Apub let String.slice (s: String) (start: Int) (end: Int): String = Array.concat_all(Array.slice(chars_of(s), start, end))\0A/// The string joined to itself `n` times. `n` of zero or less gives\0A/// an empty string.\0A/// Repeats `s` `n` times.\0A///\0A/// A LOOP with a self-rebinding accumulator, not recursion. It was\0A/// `s.concat(String.repeat(s, n - 1))`, which concatenates onto a\0A/// string one shorter each time, so the bytes allocated are\0A/// 1+2+3+...+n: quadratic. 25k characters allocated 313MB, 50k\0A/// allocated 1.25GB, 100k allocated 5.0GB, and a million never\0A/// finished.\0A///\0A/// `out = out.concat(s)` is the shape the backend can grow in place,\0A/// because the slot is overwritten with the result and nothing can\0A/// observe a mutation. Found while writing SHA-256, whose test\0A/// vector needs a million characters.\0Apub let String.repeat (s: String) (n: Int): String = {\0A    let mut out = \22\22;\0A    for i in 0..n { out = out.concat(s); };\0A    out\0A}\0A\0A/// The number of CHARACTERS in `s`, which is not `s.len()`.\0A///\0A/// `.len()` is bytes; everything else in this section is codepoints.\0A/// The two agree on ASCII and part company on anything else, so a\0A/// program that lines text up in columns wants this one; padding\0A/// computed from a byte count puts an accented name in the wrong place.\0A/// The number of CHARACTERS (codepoints). `String.len` is the number\0A/// of BYTES, and the two differ for anything outside ASCII.\0Apub let String.char_len (s: String): Int = chars_of(s).len()\0A\0A/// Pad `s` to `width` CHARACTERS with `fill`, on the left or the right.\0A///\0A/// A `fill` that is